'''''Salix pulchra''''' is a species of flowering plant in the [[Salicaceae|willow family]], known by the common names '''diamondleaf willow''', '''tealeaf willow''', and '''thin red willow'''.<ref name=feis>Uchytil, Ronald J. 1991. [http://www.fs.fed.us/database/feis/plants/shrub/salpul/all.html ''Salix pulchra''.] In: Fire Effects Information System, [Online]. U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Rocky Mountain Research Station, Fire Sciences Laboratory.</ref> It is native to northern North America, where it occurs in [[Alaska]], [[Yukon]], the [[Northwest Territories]], and [[Nunavut]]. The species is also found in northern [[British Columbia]], and occurs in [[Russia]].<ref name=fna>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=242445844 ''Salix pulchra''.] ''[[Flora of North America]]''.</ref>

This [[willow]] is an erect, deciduous [[shrub]] growing up to {{convert|4.6|m|ft|abbr=on}} tall, but usually remaining smaller, especially in the [[Arctic]] and in [[alpine climate]]s.<ref name=feis/> Along with [[Salix richardsonii|''S. richardsonii'']] and [[Salix alaxensis|''S. alaxensis'']], it is one of the tallest growing willows in the [[Canadian Arctic Archipelago]].<ref name="Nature.ca">{{cite web|url=https://nature.ca/aaflora/data/www/wlsapu.htm |title=''Salix pulchra'' Cham. |work= Flora of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago: Descriptions, Illustrations, Identification, and Information Retrieval |author=Aiken, S.G. |author2=Dallwitz, M.J. |author3=Consaul, L.L. |author4=McJannet, C.L. |author5=Boles, R.L. |author6=Argus, G.W. |author7=Gillett, J.M. |author8=Scott, P.J. |author9=Elven, R. |author10=LeBlanc, M.C. |author11=Gillespie, L.J. |author12=Brysting, A.K. |author13=Solstad, H. |author14=Harris, J.G. |name-list-style=amp |publisher=[[National Research Council of Canada]] |year=2007 |accessdate=2012-03-23}}</ref> The species is [[plant sexuality|dioecious]], with male and female reproductive parts occurring on separate individuals. The [[inflorescence]] is a [[catkin]]. The catkins grow before the leaves appear in the spring.<ref name=feis/> The leaves are green above and whitish underneath, and hairless.<ref name="Collet2004">{{cite web |url=http://alaska.fws.gov/nwr/yukonflats/pdf/WillowsFinal.pdf |title=Willows of Interior Alaska |publisher=[[United States Fish and Wildlife Service|US Fish & Wildlife Service]] |author=Collet, Dominique M. |year=2004 |access-date=2012-03-23 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111016113346/http://alaska.fws.gov/nwr/yukonflats/pdf/WillowsFinal.pdf |archive-date=2011-10-16 }}</ref>

This plant is a [[dominance (ecology)|dominant]] species on the [[tundra]] in many areas. It commonly grows with [[carex|sedges]]. It may form [[thicket]]s with other species of willow along waterways and on [[floodplain]]s. It grows in [[picea|spruce]] woodlands and [[muskeg]]s. It grows above the [[tree line|timberline]] in interior Alaska, and at the timberline in northern Alaska. It often sprouts and grows after [[wildfire]], and it persists in open habitat.<ref name=feis/>

This species was formerly known as ''S. planifolia'' subsp. ''pulchra'', a subspecies of [[Salix planifolia|''S. planifolia'']],<ref name=feis/> but has since been treated as a distinct species.<ref name="Nature.ca"/> The two willow species have overlapping distribution and similar appearance, but can be distinguished by the longer stipules in ''S. pulchra''.<ref name="Argus2004">{{cite web |url=http://137.229.141.57/wp-content/uploads/2011/02/GuideSalixAK-YT11May05.pdf |title=A Guide to the identification of Salix (willows) in Alaska, the Yukon Territory and adjacent regions |work=Workshop on willow identification |author=Argus, George W. |year=2004 |access-date=2012-03-25 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305000157/http://137.229.141.57/wp-content/uploads/2011/02/GuideSalixAK-YT11May05.pdf |archive-date=2016-03-05 }}</ref> In areas where both willows occur, ''S. pulchra'' is often found at high elevations.<ref name="Argus2004"/> ''S. pulchra'' and ''S. planifolia'' also form natural hybrids.<ref name=fna/>

''Salix pulchra'' is one of the least bitter-tasting willows in Alaska, and in the springtime, young leaves may be harvested for human consumption.<ref name="Collet2004"/> The leaves and shoots were eaten by the [[Inuit]] raw and dried, or stored in [[pinniped|seal oil]] for future use. The leaves were also used to brew [[herbal tea|tea]] and make soup.<ref name=ethno>[http://herb.umd.umich.edu/herb/search.pl?searchstring=Salix+pulchra ''Salix pulchra''.] University of Michigan Ethnobotany.</ref> At [[Nunivak Island]], parts of this plant were chewed to treat ailments such as mouth sores and pain.<ref name="Smith1973">{{cite journal|last=Smith |first=G. Warren|year=1973 |title=Arctic Pharmacognosia|journal=Arctic |volume=26|issue=4|pages=324–333 (p. 325)|url=http://pubs.aina.ucalgary.ca/arctic/Arctic26-4-324.pdf|accessdate=2012-03-25 |doi=10.14430/arctic2931}}</ref> This willow also provides a good source of [[vitamin C]].<ref name="Halderson">{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SIXYAAAAMAAJ&q=pulchra |title=Alaska native food practices, customs, and holidays |page=20 |author=Halderson, Karen |publisher=American Dietetic Association |year=1991 |isbn=9780880911009 |accessdate=2012-03-26}}</ref>

Apart from medicinal purposes, [[Native Americans in the United States|Native Americans]] used parts of willows, including this species, in [[basket weaving]], to make bows and arrows, and for building animal traps.<ref name=feis/>

